## 2. Design decisions (what, and where it departs from IterationT 3.2.0)
ITT reference read: Lib/IndividualFounctions/EndSky.glsl (CalculateStars, PlanetEnd2,
BlackHole_AccretionDisc_Stars, EndFog) plus world1/composite.fsh lines 246-248. Only the
ideas/structure were taken; none of its expressions or constant tables are used.

(a) Star field - layered magnitudes + a real colour-temperature spread
  ITT: one 3D cell hash at scale 384, coverage 0.007, luminance <= 0.04, blackbody
  4000-8000 K, one population only. Astra keeps its pre-existing per-cube-face
  projection (256x256x6 cells: no volumetric swirl, no corner pinch) and adds a dense
  faint field (cell threshold 0.32) plus a separate sparse bright layer (0.012, i.e.
  27x rarer) with a fixed halo fraction, a rank^5 power law inside each population, and
  a Planckian hue spanning 3000-12000 K with the warm minority fixed at one star in
  six. Net effect vs ITT: a ~7x denser field whose brightness distribution is a power
  law rather than one band, and whose colour is a temperature continuum rather than a
  narrow 4000-8000 K window.

(b) Interstellar medium - a great-circle band with dust lanes
  ITT has no interstellar medium at all (point stars, a planet, a disc). The old Astra
  END_NEBULA term was a single smoothstep blip. It is now one band with its own normal
  (deliberately NOT the ring axis, so band and ring interleave instead of forming one
  tilted plane), three cloud octaves, a separate lower-frequency field subtracted as
  dark dust lanes, and a cold/warm mix driven by the view direction. It returns exactly
  zero away from its great circle, so no full-sky haze is introduced (design section 6).

(c) Accretion disc - a temperature run and a concentrated emissivity law
  ITT: Blackbody(2700 + glowStrength*50) inside a 1/(gr^4*400) glow, plus a separate
  hard-coded Blackbody(12000) torus bloom. Astra now runs the hue from 9000 K (inner)
  to 3200 K (outer) through its own Planckian helper and steepens the radial emission
  law (3.0 -> 0.45 across u^0.7) relative to the density profile, so the brightest
  annulus is the inner edge instead of the disc being uniformly lit. The Doppler blue
  shift stays a separate, weaker term so it cannot be mistaken for the ramp.

(d) Photon ring - width tied to the pixel footprint
  The half-width was max(radians(0.055)=0.055 deg, cone*0.5): the footprint term was
  smaller than the fixed term on a coarse target and larger on a fine one, so the same
  line was ~2 px at one output resolution and sub-pixel at the next. It is now
  max(radians(0.014)=0.014 deg, cone*1.2), i.e. ~1.2 pixels at every resolution. Peak
  amplitude 1.8 -> 2.2, halo radius 0.65 -> 0.42 deg (a converging glow). Measured
  FWHM 0.39 deg at the 2048x1536 probe footprint against a 7.02 deg silhouette radius.

(e) Ring texture - a standing two-armed spiral density wave
  ITT ring = one octave-noise band inside PlanetEnd2. Astra already had ring gaps and
  differential shear; this adds a single spiral term wound with radius and fixed in the
  disc frame (structure without added motion), at 24% amplitude.

(f) bh_Blackbody replaced (acceptance 5)
  The IterationT CIE-spline + xyzToSrgb port is gone. Replaced by astra_planck_band /
  astra_blackbody_rgb: Planck law at three wavelengths per channel (610/545/465 nm),
  three-point Gauss-Legendre over a 52 nm Gaussian band, normalised to unit peak. The
  only physical constant is c2 = hc/k; no spline coefficients, no colour matrix and no
  reference constant table. It is live code: it supplies the star hues and the disc
  temperature run. THIRD-PARTY.md rows 226-250 were corrected in place.

